          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A claim came back asking for more specificity on a three-character code. Is that always a reasonable request?",
          "answer": "Requests for more specificity on three-character ICD-10 codes can be considered reasonable, depending on the context of the claim and the requirements of the payer. Three-character codes often represent broader categories of diagnoses, and healthcare providers may need to provide more detailed four- or five-character codes that specify the diagnosis more precisely.\n\nInsurance companies and payers may require this detailed coding to ensure accurate reimbursement, align with their internal guidelines, or due to changes in coding standards. If a claim was flagged for requiring more specificity, it's usually to meet these detailed reporting standards.\n\nIn summary, it is not unusual for claims to require further detail on three-character codes, and such requests can be considered reasonable in the context of compliance and accuracy in billing.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
